1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:02,120
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:12,360
We were out camping last weekend in the middle of the winter and it was cold.

3
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:15,120
Oh, how cold was it? I'll tell you how cold it was.

4
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:19,720
We boiled a pot of water over the campfire and we put it down on the ground

5
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:23,720
and it froze so fast that it was still a little warm.

6
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:27,240
Yeah, that's cold.

7
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:30,659
Hey, this is podcast number 51..

8
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:05,000
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

9
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:05,960
This is Clark. Green

10
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:09,060
Had a good week and we heard from a lot of folks.

11
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:11,780
Hi, Clark, Your blog is so cool.

12
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:13,700
Well, thank you.

13
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:16,440
Just dropping by to say: keep up the great work.

14
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:17,780
And that was from Lebo.

15
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:18,860
Thank you, Lebo.

16
00:01:19,180 --> 00:01:20,060
Thanks for getting in touch

17
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:23,180
Over on iTunes, Thundering Thor wrote this.

18
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:27,780
I think we're going to have a contest for the most unlikely iTunes username,

19
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:33,640
But Thundering Thor said: whatever you do regarding scouting podcasts, this is the one to sign up for.

20
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:40,340
Scoutmaster Clark delivers nuanced advice as well as produces a polished podcast show.

21
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:44,020
It's got production value, valuable information and a great listen.

22
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:45,680
Great work, Mr Green.

23
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,680
How can anyone not like a good polka?

24
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,100
Ah well, thank you very much, Thor,

25
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:54,100
And your check is in the mail. naturally,

26
00:01:55,040 --> 00:02:01,740
I love the podcast, but the 1,001 strings is a little much to bear on repeated listenings.

27
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:08,259
This is from Mark, who got in touch by email this past week.

28
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:18,640
Yeah, Mark, if you are a regular listener, at the end of the show I've got a piece of music by the 1,001 strings

29
00:02:19,019 --> 00:02:22,180
And you have to be a certain age to remember the 1,001 strings.

30
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:24,140
Think elevator music,

31
00:02:24,359 --> 00:02:27,540
Think going to the dentist and hearing this music in the background.

32
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:30,940
And it's a take on the bugle call taps.

33
00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:32,840
It's called Taps for the Fallen Brave.

34
00:02:33,359 --> 00:02:35,880
It's a pretty good piece of music, but it's a little 70s.

35
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:37,840
It's a lot 70s.

36
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:39,800
It's got harpsichords and things like that.

37
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:46,079
So Mark was kindly requesting that I might want to change that out every once in a while.

38
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:50,040
Well, Mark, listen all the way through.

39
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:53,240
I think you'll be happy when we get to the end of the podcast.

40
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:56,220
A lot of people are happy when we get to the end of the podcast.

41
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:58,340
Other than that, Mark goes on to say:

42
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:06,740
I've learned a lot from the podcast and almost always use it as the basis for my Scoutmasters Minute, with proper attribution, of course.

43
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:09,720
Well, I should hope so, Mark, because I have agents everywhere

44
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:11,580
Heard from Blaze this week

45
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:14,680
And we're having an ongoing email discussion.

46
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:17,080
Blaze is a new Scoutmaster,

47
00:03:17,420 --> 00:03:18,780
About to be a new Scoutmaster

48
00:03:18,940 --> 00:03:19,700
Weeblos Den Leader.

49
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:24,280
We're just kind of talking back and forth a little bit about getting a troop started.

50
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,859
Next week I will have the email newsletter ready to go out

51
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:36,540
And I'm sure that you'll want to get on the list for that

52
00:03:36,859 --> 00:03:40,620
And you can do that by going to scoutmastertypepadcom.

53
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:46,120
If you look in the upper right-hand corner in the right column at that web page

54
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:48,700
you will see a place where you can sign up one of two ways.

55
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:54,700
You can sign up to get an email update every time there is an update to the Scoutmaster blog

56
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:56,740
And you'll also get the newsletter.

57
00:03:57,300 --> 00:04:00,680
Or you can just sign up for the newsletter, which will come out about once a month,

58
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:07,580
And that newsletter will be a digest of what's been on the blog and in the podcast for the past month.

59
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:15,800
It will also have some interesting resources and things like that around a central scouting theme of one subject or another

60
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:22,180
And it will have a gift offer from one of my affiliate sponsors-

61
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:24,380
And I've got a good one lined up for this month-

62
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:33,040
And I'll hope you subscribe to the newsletter and get that in your email inbox very soon.

63
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:36,500
So in this podcast we're going to tackle a couple of things.

64
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:39,640
First of all, in Scoutmastership, in seven minutes or less,

65
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:44,860
we're going to return to that scarred landscape of Eagle Scout stuff.

66
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:46,320
Oh, my goodness,

67
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:51,440
I get emails regularly about different problems that people are having

68
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:55,060
with the Eagle Scout process,

69
00:04:55,340 --> 00:05:00,900
And so I'm going to talk a little bit about that in Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

70
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:06,960
And then there's an email that I received with a question about the composition of patrols,

71
00:05:07,180 --> 00:05:09,020
And we'll be talking about that afterwards.

72
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:11,360
That's a lot to do for this week's podcast.

73
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:12,880
So let's get started, shall we?

74
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:17,260
Let's get to the Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

75
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:30,120
Here's an email that I've removed all the personally identifying information from,

76
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:37,540
But it could be any one of several emails that I receive on a regular basis about this same subject.

77
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,180
Dear Mr Green, please help.

78
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:46,700
My 17-and-a-half-year-old Life Scout is working feverishly on his Eagle project.

79
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:48,580
with five months to go before his birthday,

80
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:53,359
His Scoutmaster now says he doesn't remember my son having leadership positions

81
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:57,539
And if he did, he doesn't remember his activity being sufficient.

82
00:05:58,940 --> 00:06:03,960
He is saying that my son will not earn Eagle because he hasn't fulfilled the leadership requirement

83
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:07,260
and claims that he just hasn't been a leader.

84
00:06:08,299 --> 00:06:10,020
This is very frustrating for us all.

85
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:16,020
My son is soft-spoken, but has done what he has been asked to do

86
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:18,960
and fulfilled those rules to the best of his ability.

87
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:28,460
Furthermore, this Scoutmaster says that he requires all boys to attend at least 75% of the meetings

88
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:30,820
during this six-month period of leadership.

89
00:06:31,580 --> 00:06:33,799
Since there isn't six months left before his birthday,

90
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:37,160
and because my son hasn't attended a high enough percentage of these meetings

91
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:39,480
while he has held these positions-

92
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:44,280
now don't ask me how the Scoutmaster knows his attendance wasn't high enough

93
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:46,160
or his performance wasn't good enough

94
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:49,360
if he doesn't even remember my son was holding these positions.

95
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:55,140
but can a Scoutmaster or a troop set its own standards for Eagle Scout rank advancement?

96
00:06:55,140 --> 00:06:59,000
He points to our troop handbook regarding attendance

97
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,940
and his right to be the subjective authority on that subject.

98
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:04,800
Is this fair?

99
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:09,440
Is it within the regulations for the National Boy Scouts of America?

100
00:07:10,479 --> 00:07:13,940
Can there be an attendance requirement to the six months of leadership position

101
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:15,860
associated with the Eagle rank advancement?

102
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:23,200
My son certainly meets the definition of active as it is officially defined,

103
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:26,260
and it's really actually quite a low bar to clear.

104
00:07:27,159 --> 00:07:31,840
To make matters worse, the first I ever heard that my son was in this situation

105
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:34,039
was an email from the Scoutmaster to me

106
00:07:34,039 --> 00:07:37,039
exactly five months before his birthday

107
00:07:37,039 --> 00:07:39,360
and the Scoutmaster said that was it.

108
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:41,539
There's nothing more now that could be done,

109
00:07:41,539 --> 00:07:43,880
because there just was not time.

110
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:47,740
That part of it made me feel as though we had been ambushed

111
00:07:47,740 --> 00:07:49,740
from people I have known for 10 years.

112
00:07:50,419 --> 00:07:52,300
It is just unexplainable to me.

113
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:55,240
I feel it's probably much too late to switch troops.

114
00:07:55,540 --> 00:07:58,080
I mean, we've been with this one since he was a Tiger Cup.

115
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:01,480
If I can't resolve this within the troop, what should I do?

116
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:05,880
Is there someone I should speak to at the national office or my council office?

117
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:08,580
This whole situation just makes me sick.

118
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:12,180
My oldest son is an Eagle who was dealing with the same people,

119
00:08:12,180 --> 00:08:14,640
but they didn't have any such requirements at the time.

120
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:18,980
My younger son, who am I talking about here, is not a slacker.

121
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:22,040
He's a wonderful, intelligent, respectful young man

122
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:25,940
who had a 90% attendance record for years when he was younger

123
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:32,140
and that declined a bit given his very heavy, non-scouting senior year of high school

124
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:35,600
College applications in the fall of 2010

125
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:39,039
and he spent six weeks in Kenya building houses the last two summers.

126
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:42,340
He's rowing crew in the fall and spring and all of that.

127
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:47,480
So that came to me in an email and it just frosts my cork

128
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:50,380
And I get this kind of thing regularly.

129
00:08:51,420 --> 00:08:52,380
It's amazing.

130
00:08:53,020 --> 00:08:54,480
I just cannot believe it.

131
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:59,480
So let's look at this from a couple of perspectives, shall we?

132
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:05,140
First of all, all the questions that were asked to me in that email boil down to two things:

133
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:09,360
Can attendance metrics be mandated by a troop?

134
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:12,699
In other words, can a troop have a policy that says,

135
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:14,860
in order for you to be considered an active scout,

136
00:09:15,180 --> 00:09:20,640
you have to attend a percentage of outings or a percentage of meetings, or something like that?

137
00:09:21,819 --> 00:09:24,100
Well, the answer to that question is really really easy:

138
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:24,660
No,

139
00:09:25,819 --> 00:09:27,340
A troop cannot do that.

140
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:32,120
If you look, you're going to find how the word active is defined officially

141
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:36,460
And basically that definition is: if a scout is on the roster

142
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:41,580
and he's regularly engaged by troop leadership, he is considered active.

143
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:47,640
Now I'm not going to take the time right now to argue about whether you like that policy or not.

144
00:09:48,420 --> 00:09:49,600
Some people do,

145
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:50,720
Some people don't-

146
00:09:51,740 --> 00:09:55,380
And I'm not going to take the time right now to argue why that policy is good.

147
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:57,140
I like that policy.

148
00:09:57,320 --> 00:09:58,180
I think it is good.

149
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:00,500
You know you might have a different opinion,

150
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:03,800
But it is exactly what the rules of the game are.

151
00:10:04,820 --> 00:10:09,260
You might not like the idea that you know you get six points for a touchdown

152
00:10:09,260 --> 00:10:12,700
and then you get another point for the kick. afterwards

153
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:16,300
You might not think that that's such a great idea, but that's the rules of the game

154
00:10:17,140 --> 00:10:18,760
And we have to play by the rules of the game.

155
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:20,300
We don't get to make them ourselves.

156
00:10:20,780 --> 00:10:25,280
The other question is: can a scoutmaster decide at this point

157
00:10:25,280 --> 00:10:29,240
that a scout has not shown adequate leadership?

158
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:35,880
I've got to say 99 and 99. one hundredths percent of the time, no.

159
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:42,300
I can't imagine a situation where a scoutmaster would just simply say no.

160
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:44,740
And here's why:

161
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:48,600
Because, based on the evidence that I see in this email, the boy has an Eagle Project

162
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:50,220
And he's working on it.

163
00:10:50,220 --> 00:10:55,060
So somebody had to go through the Eagle Project process with him.

164
00:10:55,560 --> 00:11:01,000
The scoutmaster had to sign that he was qualified and had reviewed this with the boy.

165
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:09,700
And if a scoutmaster has waited this long to raise concerns about the viability of a scout's leadership,

166
00:11:10,100 --> 00:11:12,360
if he's waited this long, he's not doing his job.

167
00:11:12,440 --> 00:11:14,580
He's not being a very responsible scoutmaster.

168
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:20,360
If I had concerns about a boy who was in a leadership position within the troop

169
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:25,760
and did not execute that leadership position in a way that I thought was effective,

170
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:27,740
I would talk to him immediately.

171
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:35,780
I would not wait until it was time for me to pass off his active leadership role towards his Eagle rank.

172
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:44,040
If I was uncertain that he had been in a leadership position and I had some doubts about the way that he did it

173
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:49,520
and we actually got to this point. well, my next action would be to talk to the scout himself

174
00:11:49,520 --> 00:11:54,040
and to ask him to tell me how he fulfilled the leadership requirement.

175
00:11:54,900 --> 00:11:58,560
Now, if I found his explanation wasn't very good, I would ask my senior patrol leader,

176
00:11:59,079 --> 00:12:00,480
I would ask my fellow adults,

177
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:03,480
I would ask some of my older scouts.

178
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:06,320
I would say: evaluate his leadership for me,

179
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:07,200
Tell me what you think.

180
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:13,200
I might even ask the scout's parents to help me fill in the blanks if I'm not too sure about what was going on.

181
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:18,980
What I would be doing is I would be starting from the assumption of two things:

182
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:25,800
that a scout is trustworthy and that I am on his side trying to help him sort this out.

183
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:32,220
I'm not sitting on Mount Olympus with the keys to the kingdom in my hand

184
00:12:32,220 --> 00:12:38,580
waiting for a scout to come supplicating to me about passing him on a leadership requirement.

185
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:44,300
Boys who are in leadership should have an ongoing relationship with their adult leaders

186
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:48,240
in which they are continuously discussing this leadership role,

187
00:12:48,940 --> 00:12:50,680
where they're continually being mentored

188
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:58,459
And I'm just dumbfounded that anyone would have this kind of a diseased ego

189
00:12:58,459 --> 00:13:03,459
where they think that they're going to just close the door on a kid.

190
00:13:04,459 --> 00:13:09,359
a kid who, by you know- and of course I'm hearing from the boy's father in this email,

191
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:13,160
So I understand that I'm getting one side of the story-

192
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:20,120
But who would close the door on a boy who has been very involved in things

193
00:13:20,640 --> 00:13:22,820
You know? he's a senior in high school,

194
00:13:23,260 --> 00:13:24,720
He's very involved in stuff,

195
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:25,580
He's volunteered,

196
00:13:26,300 --> 00:13:28,460
He's done a lot of things in the community,

197
00:13:28,660 --> 00:13:29,400
He's active.

198
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:32,360
Why in the world wouldn't I want him to be an Eagle Scout?

199
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:37,920
If it was just a matter of punching a ticket, punching a time clock

200
00:13:38,340 --> 00:13:41,319
whenever he showed up at a troop meeting or an outing,

201
00:13:41,660 --> 00:13:45,900
that would be a very narrow definition of what leadership is

202
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:49,240
and what being a community servant leader is.

203
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:56,600
There is more to consider than just what is within the scope of what a young man does in scouting.

204
00:13:56,600 --> 00:14:01,740
Now, yes, they have to occupy a position of responsibility for a given amount of time,

205
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:03,120
But that's pretty simple,

206
00:14:03,660 --> 00:14:04,600
That's pretty easy.

207
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:10,140
I think there are two options for anybody who finds themselves in this position:

208
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:13,460
They can fight or they can switch.

209
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:20,500
Now, the fight option is not a very good idea because it's very trying and very emotional

210
00:14:20,920 --> 00:14:25,040
And it adds more emotion to the situation that this family already finds itself in.

211
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:31,260
It means that they need to go to the chartered organization representative for their son's troop.

212
00:14:31,680 --> 00:14:36,459
They need to outline exactly what has happened, exactly why they think it is wrong,

213
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:40,199
And then the chartered organization representative needs to go to the scoutmaster

214
00:14:40,199 --> 00:14:47,260
and tell him that he needs to sign the boy off on his requirements and institute a board of review.

215
00:14:47,260 --> 00:14:54,340
If the scoutmaster is this sure that the boy does not deserve to become an Eagle Scout,

216
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:58,340
he can go up here at the board of review and let the board of review figure that one out.

217
00:14:59,720 --> 00:15:05,920
I mean, wouldn't that be just a smarter way of doing things rather than a scoutmaster just slamming the door in a boy's face?

218
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:11,699
And the reason we go to the chartered organization representative is the chartered organization representative

219
00:15:11,699 --> 00:15:15,520
has the absolute final authority on who is going to be a volunteer with the troop.

220
00:15:16,479 --> 00:15:21,620
They're the person who can tell a recalcitrant scoutmaster that his services are no longer required.

221
00:15:22,300 --> 00:15:26,800
And if this one did not kind of see the light and sign the boy's requirements

222
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:31,360
and I was the chartered organization representative, I would shake his hand, I would thank him for his service

223
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:36,540
and I would say that his services were no longer required, that we'll be appointing a new scoutmaster

224
00:15:36,540 --> 00:15:40,620
who's willing to do things the way that scouting is supposed to happen.

225
00:15:41,420 --> 00:15:48,220
Failing getting the chartered organization representative to do this, it's time to walk away and find another troop.

226
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:52,400
It's a fool's errand to think that you're going to change people's minds

227
00:15:52,740 --> 00:15:54,860
or that you're going to get them to see the light,

228
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:59,620
or that you're going to instruct them on the way that the BSA is supposed to happen,

229
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:01,700
And that's not going to work.

230
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:06,080
Outside of the chartered organization representative is going to do it. it's not going to work.

231
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:09,520
Nobody at the national office is going to get involved.

232
00:16:09,620 --> 00:16:12,440
Nobody at the council office is going to get involved, and rightfully so.

233
00:16:13,080 --> 00:16:18,740
You know, unless there is a problem that needs to come to the attention of the civil authorities,

234
00:16:19,480 --> 00:16:24,160
you are not going to get people on the council or the district level

235
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:26,560
in trying to tell a scoutmaster exactly what to do.

236
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:30,339
They can encourage him, they can advise him, they can do that type of thing,

237
00:16:30,420 --> 00:16:32,660
but as long as he was within the civil law

238
00:16:33,619 --> 00:16:38,060
and as long as he has not flagrantly- and in my opinion. this one has

239
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:41,280
flagrantly ignored the policies of the BSA.

240
00:16:42,359 --> 00:16:44,439
nobody's going to come down on him like a ton of bricks.

241
00:16:44,939 --> 00:16:45,979
It's just not going to happen.

242
00:16:46,459 --> 00:16:47,819
I wouldn't put myself through it,

243
00:16:47,959 --> 00:16:49,219
I wouldn't put my son through it.

244
00:16:49,619 --> 00:16:50,760
I'd go find another troop.

245
00:16:50,760 --> 00:16:57,500
If that boy showed up at my doorstep and his father was along with him,

246
00:16:57,800 --> 00:17:02,540
it would take us about 10 minutes to sit down and figure out if he had been in a leadership position or not.

247
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:04,359
Very simple questions,

248
00:17:04,680 --> 00:17:05,540
Very easy to do,

249
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:08,780
And if there was any doubt in my mind,

250
00:17:08,960 --> 00:17:11,319
I could call a couple of people from his old troop,

251
00:17:11,619 --> 00:17:12,859
ask them the same questions

252
00:17:13,319 --> 00:17:14,339
and then I would know.

253
00:17:14,339 --> 00:17:18,839
Then I would make sure to sign off his requirements.

254
00:17:19,740 --> 00:17:21,159
I would institute,

255
00:17:21,780 --> 00:17:22,459
I would, you know,

256
00:17:22,540 --> 00:17:25,339
get the wheels in motion for an Eagle Border review

257
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:27,260
and that boy would become an Eagle Scout.

258
00:17:27,699 --> 00:17:31,919
No matter what option this family decides to pursue,

259
00:17:32,620 --> 00:17:35,000
it's going to be emotional,

260
00:17:35,379 --> 00:17:36,260
it's going to be difficult,

261
00:17:36,740 --> 00:17:39,820
it's going to color this young man's experience in scouting.

262
00:17:39,820 --> 00:17:47,220
He can't be insulated from the fact that sometimes people who he is supposed to trust and respect have let him down.

263
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:50,879
We don't let anger and revenge inform our actions. in that case

264
00:17:50,939 --> 00:17:51,679
We just move on.

265
00:17:51,919 --> 00:18:00,200
I think the real message here for us as scout leaders is to understand what the policies are surrounding a question like this

266
00:18:00,200 --> 00:18:03,460
and what best practices are surrounding a question like this.

267
00:18:03,679 --> 00:18:05,100
And I think I've already stated them,

268
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:06,260
but I'll restate this:

269
00:18:06,260 --> 00:18:15,100
Listen, you don't get to the point where you are going to sit down and ambush a boy and his family at a Scoutmasters conference

270
00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:15,740
because you say,

271
00:18:15,980 --> 00:18:19,940
well, I've been watching you for these past several months and you're just not a leader.

272
00:18:21,080 --> 00:18:22,400
That is unconscionable,

273
00:18:22,780 --> 00:18:24,960
That is mean and that is bullying.

274
00:18:25,379 --> 00:18:28,700
If I have a question about a boy's capability,

275
00:18:29,140 --> 00:18:30,460
about his dedication

276
00:18:30,840 --> 00:18:33,000
or about his suitability as a leader,

277
00:18:33,100 --> 00:18:35,340
I'm going to address it with him right away.

278
00:18:35,340 --> 00:18:37,040
I'm not going to wait

279
00:18:38,220 --> 00:18:42,540
And I'm going to have those questions, certainly about any Scout who is a leader.

280
00:18:43,300 --> 00:18:46,020
But my job, that I need to do as a Scoutmaster

281
00:18:46,020 --> 00:18:50,820
is to be working closely along with my youth leadership,

282
00:18:51,659 --> 00:18:53,199
mentoring them, talking to them

283
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:58,580
and having an ongoing relationship in which these things are discussed on a regular basis.

284
00:18:58,580 --> 00:19:06,860
So that is one story that is repeated, unfortunately, many, many, many times.

285
00:19:07,179 --> 00:19:09,100
I'm sure this bears further discussion.

286
00:19:09,580 --> 00:19:13,780
There are a lot of smart people that listen to this podcast who've been Scout leaders for a long time

287
00:19:13,780 --> 00:19:15,520
and have been up against the same thing,

288
00:19:15,560 --> 00:19:16,780
and I'm interested to hear from you.

289
00:19:17,960 --> 00:19:21,419
And yes, there are many possible scenarios and things like that.

290
00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:23,200
Yes, I only have one side of the story,

291
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:25,440
but I hear this all the time.

292
00:19:25,600 --> 00:19:27,220
If you have some thoughts on the subject

293
00:19:27,740 --> 00:19:31,340
and you think you can shed some light on how to fix this type of problem,

294
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:32,880
do get in touch.

295
00:19:32,880 --> 00:19:50,900
Hi, I'm Kevin Callen

296
00:19:50,900 --> 00:19:53,700
and I'm listening to Scoutmaster iPod.

297
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:54,400
No, iPod,

298
00:19:54,880 --> 00:19:55,800
What is it called? again,

299
00:19:59,880 --> 00:20:01,840
Hi, I'm Kevin Callen, the Happy Camper,

300
00:20:01,840 --> 00:20:04,379
and you're listening to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

301
00:20:04,879 --> 00:20:06,199
Ah, beautiful, beautiful.

302
00:20:06,439 --> 00:20:08,199
Write me a letter,

303
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:10,600
Send it by mail,

304
00:20:12,220 --> 00:20:13,740
Email. that is folks.

305
00:20:14,340 --> 00:20:17,340
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

306
00:20:24,760 --> 00:20:26,020
Hey, here's another email.

307
00:20:26,860 --> 00:20:30,320
I take in the Scoutmaster Podcast and blog on a regular basis

308
00:20:30,320 --> 00:20:32,159
and I learn a lot from them.

309
00:20:32,240 --> 00:20:35,360
I was wondering if you had any thoughts on the composition of patrols,

310
00:20:35,860 --> 00:20:40,500
that is, mixed-age patrols as opposed to same-age patrols, as recommended by the BSA.

311
00:20:41,040 --> 00:20:43,240
I tend to prefer same-age patrols,

312
00:20:43,280 --> 00:20:46,800
but I have heard some strong arguments in favor of mixed-age patrols,

313
00:20:46,860 --> 00:20:50,300
namely the distribution of maturity and experience amongst the patrols,

314
00:20:50,300 --> 00:20:53,780
and that's from Jeff, who is a Scoutmaster in Benton, Arkansas.

315
00:20:53,780 --> 00:20:55,360
Thanks, Jeff, for getting in touch

316
00:20:55,620 --> 00:20:59,060
and I'm glad that you find the podcast and the blog useful.

317
00:21:00,220 --> 00:21:02,899
I've seen both the mixed-age and the same-age patrols work well.

318
00:21:03,620 --> 00:21:08,060
I'd suggest that the most important factor is how the patrols get formed in the first place.

319
00:21:09,020 --> 00:21:10,879
Let's imagine you're going to start a new troop

320
00:21:10,980 --> 00:21:14,960
and there are 35 boys you know waiting to form patrols.

321
00:21:15,460 --> 00:21:16,560
So how are you going to do that?

322
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:17,740
Well, there's a lot of alternatives.

323
00:21:17,740 --> 00:21:22,480
Perhaps a couple of adults would look over a list or a questionnaire completed by the Scouts

324
00:21:22,480 --> 00:21:25,440
and divide them into patrols according to their age or interests,

325
00:21:25,440 --> 00:21:29,200
or into combinations that you know they imagine might would work.

326
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:33,380
Another way to form patrols would be to ask the boys to do the task themselves

327
00:21:33,380 --> 00:21:34,340
and to see what happens.

328
00:21:37,060 --> 00:21:41,680
What they would be likely to do is to make up patrols based on their own interests and friendships.

329
00:21:42,660 --> 00:21:46,480
They may make some changes along the way as they figure out what works and what doesn't,

330
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:50,640
and these may end up being mixed-age patrols or same-age patrols.

331
00:21:50,700 --> 00:21:54,400
What matters is, in the end, they've done it themselves.

332
00:21:55,620 --> 00:21:59,160
I've got to tell you I used to be much more concerned about engineering patrols myself

333
00:21:59,160 --> 00:22:02,820
and to put them together the way that I thought that they would work,

334
00:22:02,900 --> 00:22:06,220
but I found that, you know, when I put this into the hands of the Scouts,

335
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:08,700
I get a much, much better result.

336
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:13,880
I'll remind everybody that Baden-Powell once said: the best progress is made in those troops

337
00:22:13,880 --> 00:22:17,580
where the power and responsibility are really put into the hands of the patrol leaders.

338
00:22:18,240 --> 00:22:20,820
This is the secret of success in scout training.

339
00:22:21,620 --> 00:22:25,800
I too have heard, and for a time subscribed to the idea that patrols would function better

340
00:22:25,800 --> 00:22:28,020
if we engineer them to distribute age and maturity.

341
00:22:28,700 --> 00:22:30,520
You know it's a pretty sensible argument,

342
00:22:31,040 --> 00:22:35,020
But in the end I don't get involved in how patrols get formed in my troop.

343
00:22:35,020 --> 00:22:38,040
I leave this totally in the hands of the patrol leaders' counsel.

344
00:22:38,720 --> 00:22:44,100
Very occasionally a parent will get in touch and tell me that their son isn't getting along very well

345
00:22:44,100 --> 00:22:48,700
with somebody else in his patrol and I'll relay that information to my senior patrol leader.

346
00:22:48,820 --> 00:22:50,980
We'll discuss it and I'll ask him to look into it,

347
00:22:51,440 --> 00:22:54,480
And usually this results in finding a better fit for the scouting question.

348
00:22:54,480 --> 00:23:03,100
But remember, when power and responsibility are really, really, really put into the hands of the patrol leaders,

349
00:23:03,480 --> 00:23:07,000
they rise to the challenge and they make good things happen.

350
00:23:07,640 --> 00:23:11,620
They'll amaze you at what they're able to do every single time.

351
00:23:18,100 --> 00:23:21,060
Well, that's all for this edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast.

